			<div class="content" id="post-content-4780099">
									

<p>Hi Ryze,</p>
<p>A few thoughts based in part on half-remembered astrophysics lectures&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What would happen if a ship maintained orbit around a spinning supermassive black hole?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In general, if you're far enough out (outside any accretion disc) and pick the right orbit there's no reason to think it wouldn't just go round and round and round and round. BH don't magically &quot;suck&quot; things in, their gravity is just as strong as anything else with the same mass, and indeed if they formed from the death of a single star their mass will be less than that of the original star.</p>
<p>You probably want to avoid accretion discs &#8212; the ring of matter (gas, dust, planets, stars, etc) spiralling into the BH. Probably best if there's no accretion disc at all, but you almost certainly don't want to be orbiting within it - risk of collisions, plus your orbit probably either won't be stable so you'll need additional station keeping, or will be constrained by magnetic fields.</p>
<p>Infalling matter means there will be bursts of radiation from time to time &#8212; high energy electromagnetic burps whenever the BH swallows an asteroid from its accretion disc. This will be most pronounced at the poles (orthogonal to the plane of the accretion disc) where anything risks getting <em>frazzled</em> by such a burst</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Two things you mention are worth pointing out, don't really affect the answers but you may want to keep them in mind&#8230;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>spinning</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's expected that all natural astrophysical black holes rotate - although there are solutions for non-rotating ones they aren't expected to occur naturally because whatever they formed from would have some amount of angular momentum that would be preserved during collapse</p>
<p>If a BH wasn't rotating then that would be a strong indicator of something weird</p>
<blockquote>
<p>supermassive</p>
</blockquote>
<p>SMBH are *big*, they're the things in the centers of galaxies, generally a minimum of 1 million solar masses and going up to billions (the Milky Way's is believed to be around 4M). BH in general are a bit weird, and SMBH are (in some ways) differently weird, for example&#8230;<br />
- the radius of a BH scales linearly with mass, and the volume of a sphere scales with the cube of the radius&#8230; so as the mass (and size) of a BH goes up the density falls&#8230; you've heard that Saturn could float in a sufficiently large pool of water, yes? So could a sufficiently large SMBH<br />
- related to that, you know the idea of people getting stretched out as they cross a BH event horizon&#8230; that's because of the gravitational gradient - gravity is getting much stronger over a short distance, so gravity is a lot stronger at your feet than it is at your head&#8230; but with a SMBH that effect is diminished, you could cross the event horizon without even noticing the gravitational gradient until you were a long way inside!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if somebody was to broadcast a message in the ergosphere?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The ergosphere is the region of space close to a rotating BH that gets dragged around as the BH spins, a bit like water gets swirled around by something rotating in it. Much like signals are &quot;bent&quot; by gravity around any high mass, so will they be &quot;dragged&quot; around in the ergosphere. note that the ergosphere depends on the rotation, so extends furthest along the plane of rotation and disappears at the poles.</p>
<p>You're going to run into problems with both directionality and degradation that I'd expect to cause huge problems for any general purpose inter-spaceship communication system, but which could probably be mitigated by having specifically designed systems and the receiving ship in a particular location relative to the sending ship. Indeed, I think (not sure) that sending/receiving would be significantly easier/harder depending on relative position - so you may want the ship outside the ergosphere to change position depending on whether its sending or receiving (maybe, really not sure)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if something faster than light flew towards a black hole?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As Reimann said - whatever you want!</p>
<p>Our understanding of how BH behave is based (in part) on General Relativity, GR assumes Special Relativity, which assumes a finite &amp; limiting speed of light. In particular &#8212; anything that has a mass cannot travel at the speed of light, anything that dosn't have a mass must travel at the speed of light, nothing can be accelerated from less than c up to c, etc.</p>
<p>So how they interact is entirely up to you, there isn't a &quot;right&quot; answer &#8212; pick what you want to achieve and hand-wave the specifics</p>
<p>For example, you might consider that there is a v&gt;c event horizon, it's just smaller than the normal v=c one so a superluminal particle could get inside the normal event horizon, as long as it dosn't get too far inside. Of course then you need to consider what's &quot;inside&quot; the EH, which is perhaps the far more complicated part!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>what are professional terms surrounding these things</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A few things to consider and/or read up on&#8230;</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">No Hair theorem</span><br />
BH are all practically identical, they have no distinguishing physical qualities, and are entirely described by just three quantities</p>
<ul>
<li>all naturally occurring BH will rotate &amp; will therefore have angular momentum</li>
<li>they have charge - but it will almost always be neutral or very close to neutral</li>
<li>other than those two they just have their mass (mass-energy)</li>
</ul>
<p>This is known as the &quot;no hair theorem&quot;, and is generally believed to be true</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Penrose process</span><br />
You can (in theory) &quot;mine&quot; energy from the ergosphere by exploiting conservation of angular momentum to reduce the angular momentum of the BH while increasing the kinetic energy of an object in the ergosphere. The usual description of this considers a rocket entering the ergosphere (jettisoning mass over the EH in such a way as to reduce the AM, then gaining KE to conserve momentum) or an orbiting space station with a tether lowered into the appropriate region of the ergosphere</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Information paradox</span><br />
Quantum mechanics really dislikes the idea that information (in general, and specifically about a wave function) can be destroyed, so what happens to the information associated with a particle when it falls into a BH? The &quot;no hair&quot; theorem says that there's no way for that information to be preserved (by encoding it within the BH). This was the subject of much debate (and a few funny bets) between some big-name physicists. The current assumptions tend to be along the lines of the information is preserved by being encoded in quantum fluctuations on the event horizon and subsequently contained in Hawking radiation (see also the Holographic Principle for more on encoding a description of an object on its boundary)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>how would you theoretically destroy one?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As Riemann mentioned, they evaporate through Hawking radiation &#8212; you'll just need to wait a few billion years for it to happen!</p>
<p>You could remove mass-energy somehow, but &quot;simply&quot; feeding it anti-matter wouldn't work as that has the same mass-energy as normal matter. Maybe that's where you could use the ftl particles you mentioned - perhaps although they couldn't be used to extract information they could be used in a similar way to the Penrose process, only rather than reducing angular momentum they reduce mass-energy</p>
<p>Or you could add angular momentum - spinning the BH faster could (in theory) cause the event horizon to disappear. Specifically, I think, the event horizon only exists if M^4 &gt; J^2 (I think). Then the question is&#8230; what's left behind when the event horizon is gone?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Hope that's helpful (and, possibly, even right!)</p>
<p><span style="font-size:smaller;"><em>love your energy friend but collapsed for our mobile readers! &lt;3 Riemann</em></span></p>

