			<div class="content" id="post-content-4777076">
									

<p>I think I was probably going to get @'d for this post eventually :p</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What would happen if a ship maintained orbit around a spinning supermassive black hole?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nothing, really. Or alot. Depends where in orbit. There <em>are</em> stable orbits around a black hole in the same way that there are stable orbits around any celestial body. I'm not sure what the radius of the orbit is, it's been a couple years since I did orbital mechanics, but it exists.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if somebody was to broadcast a message in the ergosphere?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It would probably be severely degraded <em>if</em> you managed to catch the signal. But it'd be very difficult to find that signal because it can't propagate like normal light. It'd end up looking more like a laser than a wifi. I feel like the signal would also likely be redshifted to hell, so, something to be aware of.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What if something faster than light flew towards a black hole?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Whatever the hell you want, honestly. I think it'd probably get caught, depending on the energy of the photon and the distance to the event horizon, but that's a rather boring answer, even though it's the most likely case.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also, what are professional terms surrounding these things, and how would you theoretically destroy one?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you're just looking for black hole related jargon, start reading scientific papers. Specifically you might find the no-hair theorem, information paradox, and Hawking radiation interesting here.</p>
<p>As to how to destroy one? Wait. Inexplicably, some small amounts of radiation does leak from black holes (Hawking radiation) that decreases the mass. It's pretty cool! But black holes do &quot;burn out&quot; with time.</p>

